# KNOWN SELF-HOST GAP (R38 residual) — a GENERIC-equip `&self` MUTATOR
# invoked via a NAMED receiver that is a bare-value param is NOT
# materialized by the self-host, so it WRITES THROUGH to the caller.
#
# Rust gg materializes (its `method_mutates_receiver` over-approx returns
# true for any `&self`, and `cow_before_mutation` no-ops on owned roots so
# it never balloons), producing the correct Y then A. The R38 self-host
# mutation-inference pass (`compute_method_mutates_self`) classifies
# NON-generic equips only (`type_params.len() == 0`), so `Cell__assign` —
# a generic-equip method — is absent from `method_mutates_self`; the
# named-receiver gate returns false and the write goes through (Y then Y).
#
# This is a DEGRADE-TO-BASE residual (no NEW miscompile — the pre-R38
# self-host wrote through for ALL named user-receiver mutators; R38 closed
# the NON-generic sub-case). The fix is to classify generic-equip methods
# too, mirroring the fn_sigs generic-instances pre-pass. Filed in TODO.md.
#
# Correct (language-intended, Rust) output: Y then A.
struct Cell[T]:
    T val

equip [T] Cell[T]:
    void assign(&self, T v):
        self.val = v

String touch(Cell[String] c):
    c.assign("Y")
    return c.val

void main():
    Cell[String] orig = Cell[String]("A")
    String inner = touch(orig)
    print(inner)          # Y
    print(orig.val)       # A  (Rust; self-host currently writes through -> Y)
